<Project>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Directory.Build.targets, and why this component needs one at all
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    MSBuild looks Directory.Build.targets up SEPARATELY from Directory.Build.props and
    stops at the first one it finds walking up. So the moment this file exists, a project
    under src/ or tests/ stops seeing Broiler.Browser's — including the configuration-name
    DECLARATION that file is the only carrier of. Chaining explicitly is what keeps the
    in-tree build answering exactly as it did before this component was extracted; the
    parent's own contents are two ItemGroups conditioned on Broiler.HTML project paths,
    which cannot match anything here.

    Broiler.JS learned this the expensive way. It is the one submodule that had a
    Directory.Build.targets of its own, it stopped the lookup dead, and its twenty-two
    projects compiled UNOPTIMISED under Release-VM until it was made to chain.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerParentBuildTargets>$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.targets', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))</BroilerParentBuildTargets>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)" Condition="'$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)' != ''" />

  <!--
    Making the names BEHAVE is Directory.Build.props; making them EXIST is here, and the
    two cannot share an import. This one has to be unconditional and has to be last: a
    project that spells out its own <Configurations> in the csproj body overwrites
    anything the props side set, and this is the first point that sees the result.

    `dotnet build -c Debug-VM` is content to hand a project a configuration it never
    declared, so no command line notices this missing. Visual Studio resolves a solution
    build type onto a project configuration OF THE SAME NAME and refuses when there is
    none — `Invalid project mappings. See log file`, against every project whose
    $(Configurations) is still the SDK default.

    The imported file's append is idempotent, so running after the parent's copy (which
    the chain above may already have run) adds nothing a second time.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.ConfigurationNames.targets" />

</Project>
